UPDATE companies SET description='Prysmian North America is headquartered in Lexington, South Carolina, and operates as the North American arm of the global Prysmian Group. The company maintains more than 50 facilities across the United States and employs approximately 9,000 people.

The company manufactures wire, cable, and fiber‑optic products for transmission, power‑grid, digital‑solutions, and electrification markets. Its portfolio includes high‑voltage transmission cables, submarine telecom cables, industrial fiber‑optic cable, and connectivity components for data centers. Recent expansions — a $100 million project in Jackson, TN, an $80 million project in Lexington, SC, and a more than $1 billion project in Claremont, NC — are adding domestic fiber and optical‑cable capacity. The company invests roughly $20 million annually in R&D and reports €9 billion in sales.

Prysmian serves utilities, telecom operators, data‑center builders, and industrial customers. Notable projects include supplying ELYSIS® low‑carbon aluminum cables for an Amazon data center and providing network solutions for rural broadband and industrial fiber applications. The “Made Right Here” initiative underscores a focus on U.S.‑based production across its 50‑plus locations.

The company says its differentiation comes from a broad, industry‑brand portfolio, the Encore Wire partnership that expands building‑wire and distribution capabilities, and a commitment to low‑carbon materials such as ELYSIS® aluminum. Its scale and domestic footprint are positioned to support the energy transition and growing digital‑infrastructure demand.', certifications=ARRAY[]::text[], profile='{"capabilities": ["Fiber optic cable manufacturing", "Optical cable manufacturing", "Wire and cable manufacturing", "High-voltage transmission cable production", "Submarine telecom cable production", "Industrial fiber optic cable production", "Connectivity component manufacturing", "Research and development", "Digital solutions integration", "Low-carbon aluminum cable production (ELYSIS\u00ae)"], "industries": ["Transmission", "Power Grid", "Digital Solutions", "Electrification", "Submarine Telecom", "Data Centers", "Telecommunications", "Network Solutions for Industries"], "materials": ["ELYSIS\u00ae low-carbon aluminum", "Optical fiber"], "equipment": [], "volume": "", "lead_time": "", "tolerances": "", "built_on": ["\"Made Right Here\" U.S. manufacturing commitment across 50+ facilities", "$20\u202fmillion annual R&D investment", "Encore Wire partnership expanding building\u2011wire portfolio", "First electrical cables using ELYSIS\u00ae low\u2011carbon aluminum", "Domestic fiber\u2011optic cable capacity expansions totaling over $1\u202fbillion"], "facts_used": ["Headquarters in Lexington, South Carolina -> db, /", "Approximately 9,000 employees in North America -> db, /", "More than 50 facilities across North America -> /", "Markets served: Transmission, Power Grid, Digital Solutions, Electrification -> /", "$20 million invested in R&D annually -> /", "\u20ac9 billion in sales -> /", "$100 million expansion in Jackson, TN creating 100 jobs -> /", "$80 million expansion in Lexington, SC expanding fiber and optical cable capacity -> /", "More than $1 billion expansion in Claremont, NC expanding fiber and optical cable capacity -> /", "Partnership with Encore Wire -> /", "ELYSIS\u00ae low-carbon aluminum cables for Amazon Data Center -> /", "Made Right Here initiative emphasizing U.S. manufacturing -> /", "Submarine Telecom mentioned as industry served -> industries_served", "Network Solutions for Industries mentioned -> industries_served"], "company_type": "Manufacturer", "makes_products": "yes", "type_evidence": "\"Wire and Cable Manufacturer\" and \"manufacturing innovative wire and cabling solutions right here in America\"", "founded_year": "", "employee_count": "9,000", "cage_code": "", "min_order": "", "export_compliance": [], "languages": [], "social_links": [], "ownership_tags": []}'::jsonb, updated_at=now() WHERE slug='prysmian-north-america' AND approved AND profile IS NULL;
UPDATE companies SET description='Nasoya traces its roots to 1978, when its founders began producing tofu in a converted barn in Leominster, Massachusetts.

The company manufactures a range of tofu products — silken, firm, extra-firm, and super-firm — along with Asian-inspired items such as noodles, dumplings, and wonton wraps, and a line of ready-to-eat plant-based meals under the Plantspired brand. A 1987 facility expansion pushed output to more than 60,000 pounds of tofu per week, and a new Ayer plant slated for 2025 is described as the world’s largest tofu production line. Nasoya reports annual sustainability metrics that include recycling 80 tons of cardboard, treating 34.6 million gallons of wastewater, and reusing 3.4 million pounds of soy byproduct.

Nasoya’s products are sold in more than 25,000 retail grocery locations nationwide, serving the plant-based protein, organic food, and Asian-inspired food categories. The brand also partners with culinary institutions such as the Culinary Institute of America to support student scholarships and innovation challenges.

The company emphasizes organic, non-GMO soybeans sourced from local farmers, tray-free packaging, and closed-loop water and waste systems as key differentiators. Its stated mission is to make wholesome, plant-based eating accessible for every lifestyle through sustainable practices and flavor innovation.', certifications=ARRAY[]::text[], profile='{"capabilities": ["Tofu manufacturing (silken, firm, extra-firm, super-firm)", "Plant-based meal production (Plantspired line)", "Asian-inspired food manufacturing (noodles, dumplings, wonton wraps)", "Sustainable packaging (tray-free)", "Wastewater treatment and brine recycling", "Cardboard recycling", "Soy byproduct reuse"], "industries": ["Plant-based foods", "Tofu manufacturing", "Asian-inspired foods", "Organic foods", "Retail grocery"], "materials": ["Organic soybeans", "Non-GMO soybeans"], "equipment": [], "volume": "60,000+ lbs/week (1987 figure)", "lead_time": "", "tolerances": "", "built_on": ["Organic, non-GMO soybeans sourced from local farmers", "Tray-free tofu packaging", "Brine recycling and wastewater treatment (34.6M gallons annually)", "World''s largest tofu production line (planned 2025)", "Tofu U educational platform for plant-based protein"], "facts_used": ["Founded 1978 in Leominster, MA -> /about-us", "Acquired by Pulmuone Foods in 2004 -> /about-us", "Headquarters in Ayer, MA -> db", "1987 facility producing >60,000 lbs tofu/week -> /about-us", "2025 new Ayer facility world''s largest tofu production line -> /about-us", "Sustainability metrics: 80 tons cardboard recycled, 34.6M gallons wastewater treated, 3.4M lbs soy reused annually -> /about-us", "Organic, non-GMO soybeans from local farmers -> /about-us", "Tofu U launched 2013 -> /about-us", "Plantspired line launched 2019 -> /about-us", "Tray-free packaging introduced 2024 -> /about-us", "Brine recycling and sustainable energy practices -> /about-us", "25,000+ stores nationwide -> /", "Product lines: silken, firm, extra-firm, super-firm -> /", "Asian-inspired products: noodles, dumplings, wonton wraps -> /about-us", "LinkedIn URL -> db"], "company_type": "Manufacturer", "makes_products": "yes", "type_evidence": "Nasoya began in 1978 when its founders started making tofu in a converted barn in Leominster, MA.", "founded_year": "1978", "employee_count": "", "cage_code": "", "min_order": "", "export_compliance": [], "languages": [], "social_links": ["https://www.linkedin.com/company/nasoya-foods-usa/"], "ownership_tags": []}'::jsonb, updated_at=now() WHERE slug='pulmuone-nasoya-foods-usa' AND approved AND profile IS NULL;
UPDATE companies SET description='Pure Country Inc. began in 1993 in the back of a family farm shop near Menoken, North Dakota. The founders started with screen printing, then added embroidery equipment in 1995 while operating from an office suite on Memorial Highway in Bismarck.

Today the shop provides embroidery, silk screening, vinyl lettering and decals, and a range of promotional and decorative products. All decoration processes run under one roof, supported by an in-house graphic design team that assists clients with artwork. The facility houses a modern embroidery department and what the company describes as state-of-the-art screen printing equipment, though specific machine models are not disclosed.

Customers include schools and sports teams ordering spirit wear, corporations from Fortune 500 firms to local small businesses needing branded apparel, and individuals seeking custom boat letters or vehicle decals. The company stocks and decorates garments from suppliers such as Carhartt, SanMar, Augusta Sportswear, and Otto Caps, among others.

